{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And when a man sells a dwelling house in a walled city.</b> After mentioning above ״and sells from his possession״ — that is, the field — and Scripture commanding that it go out in the Jubilee, because it is the mainstay of a person's life and from it his sustenance goes forth, it now mentions the sale of the house, which is a far greater blow indeed when one is forced to sell one's house; therefore the Torah wanted that it be redeemed within a year and the purchaser cannot hold it back." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״And when a man sells a dwelling house״ — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, who sold His house to the Chaldeans. ״A dwelling house״, as it is written (Psalms 132:13): ״כי בחר ה' בציון אוה למושב לו״. What befell it? As it is written (II Kings 25:9): ״וישרוף את בית ה' ואת בית המלך ואת כל בתי ירושלם ואת כל בית גדול שרף באש״. ״A walled city״, as it is written (ibid.): ״ואת חמות ירושלם סביב נתצו כל חיל כשדים״." "<b>The days of his redemption shall be.</b> Within two thousand years; this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'two thousand years of the Messiah.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The printed text cites II Kings 25:9 for 'עיר חומה'; the verse about the walls' destruction is at II Kings 25:10 — retained the citation as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:27.699470551Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}